#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198130 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198449 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4a5d6b1-e76a-5b33-8973-04d1d9062dc6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Orthogonality of a Two-Vector Set"}] :question/id #uuid "a77db107-d157-5063-b44a-1ca0a93be099" :question/math-academy-id "e-12951" :question/problem "![](images/20/20b1f07891dd26e4839258d3c2232368b3aebbf527a2b0f31bcdde2369fc66d4.svg)\n\nConsider the vectors above, and let $\\mathbf{Q}$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\mathbf{u}_1 \\cdot \\mathbf{u}_2 = 0$\n\nII. $\\{\\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{u}_1),\\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{u}_2)\\}$ is orthogonal\n\nIII. $\\{\\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{u}_1),\\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{u}_2)\\}$ is orthonormal" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\mathbf{Q}$ is orthogonal, we have that $\\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{x}) \\cdot \\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{y}) = \\mathbf{x} \\cdot \\mathbf{y}$ for any vectors $\\mathbf{x}$ and $\\mathbf{y}.$\n\nThe linear transformation $\\mathbf{Q}$ being orthogonal is equivalent to the following:\n\n- The matrix $Q$ of transformation $\\mathbf{Q}$ relative to any orthonormal basis (including the standard basis) of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ is orthogonal.\n\n- $|| \\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{x}) || = || \\mathbf{x} ||.$\n\n- If $\\mathbf{x} \\perp \\mathbf{y}$ then $\\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{x}) \\perp \\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{y})$ for any $\\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n.$ In general, $\\mathbf{Q}$ preserves the angle between any two vectors.\n\n- $\\mathbf{Q}$ maps any orthogonal (orthonormal) set into an orthogonal (orthonormal) set.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed,\n![](images/ad/adbd35f04ca988a406493df8f3dbda8769184868d5d85da89deb57ae18fb10b0.svg)\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, $\\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{u}_1) \\perp \\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{u}_2)$ since\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{u}_1)\\cdot\\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{u}_2) = \\mathbf{u}_1\\cdot\\mathbf{u}_2 = 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, both $\\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{u}_1)$ and $\\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{u}_2)$ are unit vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Q({u}_{1})‖ & =‖{u}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{(\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3})^{2}+(\\frac{1}{3})^{2}}=1 \\\\ ‖Q({u}_{2})‖ & =‖{u}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{(-\\frac{1}{3})^{2}+(\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3})^{2}}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198131 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198448 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "455b3713-34a3-535d-8baf-d279a0341e9f" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Angle Preserving Property of Orthogonal Linear Transformations"}] :question/id #uuid "0070a453-0237-571f-84fc-a9f58446e6a9" :question/math-academy-id "e-12954" :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{Q}$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. If ![](images/88/888a6e0af4cb0ad21e91c823ce01e3b7b44005650d8abb33b7e7cd15f5c8b1d0.svg) and ![](images/15/155d90bc4b1df2a185aed86db995a16762338cda30417ecb5a459b131e171dd8.svg), find\n\nI. $\\Vert \\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{x}) \\Vert.$\n\nII. $\\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{x}) \\cdot \\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{y}).$" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\mathbf{Q}$ is orthogonal, we have that $\\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{x}) \\cdot \\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{y}) = \\mathbf{x} \\cdot \\mathbf{y}$ for any vectors $\\mathbf{x}$ and $\\mathbf{y}.$\n\nThe linear transformation $\\mathbf{Q}$ being orthogonal is equivalent to the following:\n\n- The matrix $Q$ of transformation $\\mathbf{Q}$ relative to any orthonormal basis (including the standard basis) of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ is orthogonal.\n\n- $|| \\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{x}) || = || \\mathbf{x} ||.$\n\n- If $\\mathbf{x} \\perp \\mathbf{y}$ then $\\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{x}) \\perp \\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{y})$ for any $\\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n.$ In general, $\\mathbf{Q}$ preserves the angle between any two vectors.\n\n- $\\mathbf{Q}$ maps any orthogonal (orthonormal) set into an orthogonal (orthonormal) set.\n\nIn this case, since $\\mathbf{Q}$ is orthogonal, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Q(x)‖ & =‖x‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, $\\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{x}) \\cdot \\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{y}) = \\mathbf{x} \\cdot \\mathbf{y}.$ As a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x)\\cdot Q(y) & =x\\cdot y \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot 3+(-2)\\cdot 0+2\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485595 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198449 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4a5d6b1-e76a-5b33-8973-04d1d9062dc6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Orthogonality of a Two-Vector Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ec03f18-13be-55eb-92af-32d5b84aa90d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ and $W$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485126} {:answer/id #uuid "9902057c-da91-5c74-b86e-dd54d2418d30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$V$ and $W$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485146} {:answer/id #uuid "ae328ea7-ac34-5c69-a500-80ec39d884b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U,V,$ and $W$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485193} {:answer/id #uuid "b8fa2677-4376-5029-b220-a56e49c627ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485216} {:answer/id #uuid "e17db08c-8042-5528-9797-e2d47b07097c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$V$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485294}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b8fa2677-4376-5029-b220-a56e49c627ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485216} :answer-field/id #uuid "bbd54c08-5bbd-5945-9a88-65da2698583c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485448}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fd59782d-c853-5fe4-812d-f8b2814488a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-140567" :question/problem "Let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Which of the following are orthogonal sets?\n\nI. $U=\\{Q(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}),Q(\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix})\\}$\n\nII. $V=\\{Q(\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ \\sqrt{5} \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}),Q(\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ \\sqrt{5}\\end{bmatrix}),Q(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix})\\}$\n\nIII. $W=\\{Q(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}),Q(\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix})\\}$" :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nLet's denote the elements of $U,W,$ and $V$ respectively as \n\n$\\,$ $\\{Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2})\\},\\,$ $\\{Q({v}_{1}),Q({v}_{2}),Q({v}_{3})\\},\\,$ and $\\,\\{Q({w}_{1}),Q({w}_{2})\\}.$\n\nThe sets $U,V,$ and $W$ are orthogonal if the following conditions on each set hold:\n\n- $Q({u}_{1})\\cdot Q({u}_{2})=0.$\n\n- $Q({v}_{1})\\cdot Q({v}_{2})=0,\\,Q({v}_{1})\\cdot Q({v}_{3})=0,\\,Q({v}_{2})\\cdot Q({v}_{3})=0.$\n\n- $Q({w}_{1})\\cdot Q({w}_{2})=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- For the set $U,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q({u}_{1})\\cdot Q({u}_{2}) & ={u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}=-4+25=21\\ne 0.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $U$ is not an orthogonal set.\n\n- For the Set $V,$ notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q({v}_{1})\\cdot Q({v}_{2}) & ={v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ \\sqrt{5} \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ \\sqrt{5}\\end{bmatrix}=0+2\\sqrt{5}-2\\sqrt{5}=0,\\,✓ \\\\ Q({v}_{1})\\cdot Q({v}_{3}) & ={v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ \\sqrt{5} \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=-2+0-2=-4.\\,\\times  \\\\ {v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{3} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ \\sqrt{5}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=0+0+\\sqrt{5}=\\sqrt{5}\\ne 0.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $V$ is not an orthogonal set.\n\n- For the set $W,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q({w}_{1})\\cdot Q({w}_{2}) & ={w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=-10+10=0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $W$ is orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$W$ only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284748 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198449 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4a5d6b1-e76a-5b33-8973-04d1d9062dc6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Orthogonality of a Two-Vector Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a4ade3a-303a-5eec-9df3-e43d050e7127" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465651} {:answer/id #uuid "bfb5336b-989f-5a31-b653-6429b31a077f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465652}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a4ade3a-303a-5eec-9df3-e43d050e7127" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465651} :answer-field/id #uuid "50d64a07-1676-5fda-bef0-6a4849be81d2" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466669} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6722f30e-e2af-5a70-879f-cfff27425f84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465653} {:answer/id #uuid "dc3d0a9a-fffb-56ce-a7bd-59c663e5f87e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465654}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6722f30e-e2af-5a70-879f-cfff27425f84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465653} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9cb2af9-89d8-5f3d-8d83-90156885a877" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466670} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ff5d22d-d9d1-5f3d-acef-b6be4a77c7ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465655} {:answer/id #uuid "bb044ed9-c0d7-5063-b76d-362678932554" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465656}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ff5d22d-d9d1-5f3d-acef-b6be4a77c7ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465655} :answer-field/id #uuid "3cba7c41-2ad1-5047-8e7d-0cc187a92400" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466671}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1e67e934-1049-5a47-aaca-94c1d2a3bfc1" :question/math-academy-id "q-177118" :question/problem "$$\n{u}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,{u}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{u}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the vectors above, and let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0$\n- **II.** ${Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2}),Q({u}_{3})}$ is orthogonal\n- **III.** ${Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2}),Q({u}_{3})}$ is orthonormal\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false since\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}=2+12-5=9\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that, for example, $Q({u}_{1})⊥̸Q({u}_{2})$ since\n\n$$\nQ({u}_{1})\\cdot Q({u}_{2})={u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false since the set is not even orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284758 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198448 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "455b3713-34a3-535d-8baf-d279a0341e9f" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Angle Preserving Property of Orthogonal Linear Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01d44e31-35c4-58cd-b912-19a78a40c053" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281705} {:answer/id #uuid "28daa53b-997b-5300-a2ed-d7f81362d9e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282041} {:answer/id #uuid "8e58c81a-eaee-5926-9fe4-9e537a288325" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282924} {:answer/id #uuid "d2dc18cf-b08c-57d4-a41e-a0c3559bb767" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283530} {:answer/id #uuid "dd880937-04ca-5676-86cd-4fcabfdba49a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283624}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "28daa53b-997b-5300-a2ed-d7f81362d9e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282041} :answer-field/id #uuid "94778350-adf7-5edd-b7e6-c26b03c83b51" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284189}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "57add274-90e1-53e7-99ed-40cc011b2a2c" :question/math-academy-id "q-177810" :question/problem "Let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Find $‖Q(x)‖$, where $x=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2\\sqrt{3} \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nThe linear transformation $Q$ being orthogonal is equivalent to the following:\n\n- The matrix $Q$ of transformation $Q$ relative to any orthonormal basis (including the standard basis) of ${R}^{n}$ is orthogonal.\n\n- $||Q(x)||=||x||.$\n\n- If $x⊥y$ then $Q(x)⊥Q(y)$ for any $x,y\\in {R}^{n}.$ In general, $Q$ preserves the angle between any two vectors.\n\n- $Q$ maps any orthogonal (orthonormal) set into an orthogonal (orthonormal) set.\n\nIn this case, since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Q(x)‖ & =‖x‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{(-2\\sqrt{3})}^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485506 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198448 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "455b3713-34a3-535d-8baf-d279a0341e9f" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Angle Preserving Property of Orthogonal Linear Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "012bb29a-9963-57da-b90b-434c91a90ab2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484817} {:answer/id #uuid "05ab90c9-0813-586a-9b44-c98ce8be0379" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484828} {:answer/id #uuid "2e490d2d-7e97-5d30-af73-6a6f000c1b2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484912} {:answer/id #uuid "4436a127-bf8f-5d0f-b926-f265c358b489" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484964} {:answer/id #uuid "a6988062-26b2-5f46-b225-4b080bde730b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485178}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4436a127-bf8f-5d0f-b926-f265c358b489" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484964} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8b85cb0-51f8-5559-bd88-b78ed4af2dbf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485473}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "222e2ed5-e7e4-55fe-9d04-24be2fce81e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-177845" :question/problem "Let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Find $‖Q(x)‖$, where $x=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nThe linear transformation $Q$ being orthogonal is equivalent to the following:\n\n- The matrix $Q$ of transformation $Q$ relative to any orthonormal basis (including the standard basis) of ${R}^{n}$ is orthogonal.\n\n- $||Q(x)||=||x||.$\n\n- If $x⊥y$ then $Q(x)⊥Q(y)$ for any $x,y\\in {R}^{n}.$ In general, $Q$ preserves the angle between any two vectors.\n\n- $Q$ maps any orthogonal (orthonormal) set into an orthogonal (orthonormal) set.\n\nIn this case, since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Q(x)‖ & =‖x‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415156 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198449 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4a5d6b1-e76a-5b33-8973-04d1d9062dc6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Orthogonality of a Two-Vector Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81d08864-6101-5251-8351-5c0043998d07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472778} {:answer/id #uuid "1a22dbe5-0d2b-5c0a-9531-8c54bafdc81e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472779}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a22dbe5-0d2b-5c0a-9531-8c54bafdc81e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472779} :answer-field/id #uuid "71e4259d-b2ae-5c52-927d-7cf47899f776" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473784} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c84ccb50-f1be-591e-8bac-35014caba949" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472780} {:answer/id #uuid "19621db5-93e2-50ee-9ddb-8440f2d6f313" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472781}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19621db5-93e2-50ee-9ddb-8440f2d6f313" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472781} :answer-field/id #uuid "7af95f9f-6948-54ed-b3d3-37b3b7e7a68c" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473785} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b309452-f7e7-54e9-bbdf-9a3025bd85c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472782} {:answer/id #uuid "f4968354-f03a-5157-9f0d-8be8dbd50643" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472783}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5b309452-f7e7-54e9-bbdf-9a3025bd85c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472782} :answer-field/id #uuid "46245871-924f-5edd-8bb1-a6c28bc755b4" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473786}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2a7fbace-2464-5d79-be05-e6f28745c3de" :question/math-academy-id "q-178014" :question/problem "$$\n{u}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix},\\,{u}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the vectors above, and let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0$\n- **II.** ${Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2})}$ is orthogonal\n- **III.** ${Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2})}$ is orthonormal\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nThe linear transformation $Q$ being orthogonal is equivalent to the following:\n\n- The matrix $Q$ of transformation $Q$ relative to any orthonormal basis (including the standard basis) of ${R}^{n}$ is orthogonal.\n\n- $||Q(x)||=||x||.$\n\n- If $x⊥y$ then $Q(x)⊥Q(y)$ for any $x,y\\in {R}^{n}.$ In general, $Q$ preserves the angle between any two vectors.\n\n- $Q$ maps any orthogonal (orthonormal) set into an orthogonal (orthonormal) set.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=12-12=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, $Q({u}_{1})⊥Q({u}_{2})$ since\n\n$$\nQ({u}_{1})\\cdot Q({u}_{2})={u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that, for example, $Q({u}_{1})$ is not a unit vector since\n\n$$\n‖Q({u}_{1})‖=‖{u}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+(-6)^{2}}\\ne 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198978 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198448 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "455b3713-34a3-535d-8baf-d279a0341e9f" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Angle Preserving Property of Orthogonal Linear Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c5739013-0ec3-5054-9df2-0416169aee06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197739} {:answer/id #uuid "cb80e9c9-9a83-5c04-a3ab-b7735d5f125a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197740} {:answer/id #uuid "cc77a078-56f6-5c64-a1d0-37853dbff1ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197741} {:answer/id #uuid "e099e132-6017-54bd-af9b-dabda71bcfb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197742} {:answer/id #uuid "2c36f56b-9a3f-5112-a631-00ca6bd5658b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197743}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e099e132-6017-54bd-af9b-dabda71bcfb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197742} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec57d5bb-fbdf-5711-9938-796ddd4e592a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197738}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5b4e4425-10cb-5811-9da8-6b08b3f0fd92" :question/math-academy-id "q-40489" :question/problem "Let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Find $‖Q(x)‖$, where $x=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ \\sqrt{5}\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nThe linear transformation $Q$ being orthogonal is equivalent to the following:\n\n- The matrix $Q$ of transformation $Q$ relative to any orthonormal basis (including the standard basis) of ${R}^{n}$ is orthogonal.\n\n- $||Q(x)||=||x||.$\n\n- If $x⊥y$ then $Q(x)⊥Q(y)$ for any $x,y\\in {R}^{n}.$ In general, $Q$ preserves the angle between any two vectors.\n\n- $Q$ maps any orthogonal (orthonormal) set into an orthogonal (orthonormal) set.\n\nIn this case, since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Q(x)‖ & =‖x‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{4}^{2}+{(\\sqrt{5})}^{2}} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406655 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198449 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4a5d6b1-e76a-5b33-8973-04d1d9062dc6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Orthogonality of a Two-Vector Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "047c19ef-c76a-57b3-b13e-81cac8438947" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473112} {:answer/id #uuid "0f411e21-0bfc-5e60-be10-aa9385c60d56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473113}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f411e21-0bfc-5e60-be10-aa9385c60d56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473113} :answer-field/id #uuid "41b5e6b2-6fa4-5699-91d3-98491ea01274" :answer-field/key "U" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473951} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b5e99f8-6d7b-5605-8eeb-768c689a1a11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473114} {:answer/id #uuid "d3e8b7c2-03e8-52a8-8c30-1f1597b0a406" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473115}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b5e99f8-6d7b-5605-8eeb-768c689a1a11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473114} :answer-field/id #uuid "2909a2f7-876c-5e10-a289-6837f83679fc" :answer-field/key "V" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473952} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "829a1b56-ccd5-5381-aeca-6ba71098f21a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473116} {:answer/id #uuid "adcc7d05-7e81-525a-b650-a03adf317913" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473117}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "adcc7d05-7e81-525a-b650-a03adf317913" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473117} :answer-field/id #uuid "51b28bdf-55b7-5470-b1f6-bd8182431d5f" :answer-field/key "W" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473953}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9892c6c7-24de-57f2-a3e8-0b32de0c1349" :question/math-academy-id "q-40491" :question/problem "Let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Which of the following are orthogonal sets?\n- **U.** $U={Q(\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}),Q(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix})}$\n- **W.** $W={Q(\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}),Q(\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix})}$\n- **V.** $V={Q(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}),Q(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}),Q(\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix})}$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nLet's denote the elements of $U,W,$ and $V$ respectively as \n\n$\\,$ ${Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2})},\\,$ ${Q({w}_{1}),Q({w}_{2})},\\,$ and $\\,{Q({v}_{1}),Q({v}_{2}),Q({v}_{3})}.$ \n\nThe sets $U,V,$ and $W$ are orthogonal if the following conditions on each set hold:\n\n- $Q({u}_{1})\\cdot Q({u}_{2})=0.$\n\n- $Q({w}_{1})\\cdot Q({w}_{2})=0.$\n\n- $Q({v}_{1})\\cdot Q({v}_{2})=0,\\,Q({v}_{1})\\cdot Q({v}_{3})=0,\\,Q({v}_{2})\\cdot Q({v}_{3})=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- For the set $U,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q({u}_{1})\\cdot Q({u}_{2}) & ={u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}=18-18=0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $U$ is orthogonal.\n\n- For the set $W,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q({w}_{1})\\cdot Q({w}_{2}) & ={w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}=1-1=0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $W$ is orthogonal.\n\n- For the Set $V,$ notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q({v}_{1})\\cdot Q({v}_{2}) & ={v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}=2+12-5=9\\ne 0.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $V$ is not an orthogonal set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$U$ and $W$ only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406654 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198448 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "455b3713-34a3-535d-8baf-d279a0341e9f" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Angle Preserving Property of Orthogonal Linear Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24e42699-a0f0-5e81-a194-c91514b7e8ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406638} {:answer/id #uuid "46c27bd0-b639-51d1-a04d-8098da45991e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406642} {:answer/id #uuid "b3565871-fc75-5a3f-bb6e-506cbb0458f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406644} {:answer/id #uuid "bfd57b0f-3965-5b70-833f-7fa36e06ae4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406646} {:answer/id #uuid "e14e0ad1-a7b2-5023-b79c-bff39fbd35df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406649}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b3565871-fc75-5a3f-bb6e-506cbb0458f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406644} :answer-field/id #uuid "491c6e1c-81dd-5170-b618-833ce2679d93" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406651}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8a69da2c-f673-5e64-a265-9fe904a3c9ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-40514" :question/problem "Let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Find $‖Q(x)‖$, where $x=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{6} \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nThe linear transformation $Q$ being orthogonal is equivalent to the following:\n\n- The matrix $Q$ of transformation $Q$ relative to any orthonormal basis (including the standard basis) of ${R}^{n}$ is orthogonal.\n\n- $||Q(x)||=||x||.$\n\n- If $x⊥y$ then $Q(x)⊥Q(y)$ for any $x,y\\in {R}^{n}.$ In general, $Q$ preserves the angle between any two vectors.\n\n- $Q$ maps any orthogonal (orthonormal) set into an orthogonal (orthonormal) set.\n\nIn this case, since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Q(x)‖ & =‖x‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\sqrt{6})}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485518 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198449 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4a5d6b1-e76a-5b33-8973-04d1d9062dc6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Orthogonality of a Two-Vector Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c21b706-ee61-5347-b1e9-163e20da045d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$V$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484840} {:answer/id #uuid "511d2e30-3a63-5d27-8065-e177abbd52b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$V$ and $W$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484993} {:answer/id #uuid "64b5d7dc-d5a7-536d-952d-7f23ec21eaea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485035} {:answer/id #uuid "a8a7e5ba-e12b-5de2-9808-9ac0af0c0ae8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485183} {:answer/id #uuid "dcfa56ff-89e4-5c4c-b084-fe9d0a2df44d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ and $W$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485281}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "64b5d7dc-d5a7-536d-952d-7f23ec21eaea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485035} :answer-field/id #uuid "3a79a8e6-4bbb-52fc-b050-7b31066c3423" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485393}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3a3b6415-46fa-5ac6-8f1e-cce0985c5f71" :question/math-academy-id "q-40515" :question/problem "Let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Which of the following are orthogonal sets?\n\nI. $U=\\{Q(\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}),Q(\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix})\\}$\n\nII. $V=\\{Q(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}),Q(\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}),Q(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix})\\}$\n\nIII. $W=\\{Q(\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}),Q(\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix})\\}$" :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nLet's denote the elements of $U,W,$ and $V$ respectively as \n\n$\\,$ $\\{Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2})\\},\\,$ $\\{Q({v}_{1}),Q({v}_{2}),Q({v}_{3})\\},\\,$ and $\\,\\{Q({w}_{1}),Q({w}_{2})\\}.$\n\nThe sets $U,V,$ and $W$ are orthogonal if the following conditions on each set hold:\n\n- $Q({u}_{1})\\cdot Q({u}_{2})=0.$\n\n- $Q({v}_{1})\\cdot Q({v}_{2})=0,\\,Q({v}_{1})\\cdot Q({v}_{3})=0,\\,Q({v}_{2})\\cdot Q({v}_{3})=0.$\n\n- $Q({w}_{1})\\cdot Q({w}_{2})=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- For the set $U,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q({u}_{1})\\cdot Q({u}_{2}) & ={u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}=20-3=17\\ne 0.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $U$ is not and orthogonal set.\n\n- For the Set $V,$ notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q({v}_{1})\\cdot Q({v}_{2}) & ={v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}=3-4+1=0,\\,✓ \\\\ Q({v}_{1})\\cdot Q({v}_{3}) & ={v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}=2+8+18=28\\ne 0.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $V$ is not an orthogonal set.\n\n- For the set $W,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q({w}_{1})\\cdot Q({w}_{2}) & ={w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}=-15+15=0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $W$ is orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$W$ only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198979 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198448 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "455b3713-34a3-535d-8baf-d279a0341e9f" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Angle Preserving Property of Orthogonal Linear Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29e1aa1e-de86-5141-a747-49b7df88362b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324305} {:answer/id #uuid "2bb428da-53ea-533b-ad36-0e999294e68f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324318} {:answer/id #uuid "2ee62035-a9e5-5f7f-9961-5d46b2a9cb27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324345} {:answer/id #uuid "3f1e464f-a9e3-5cb7-9c86-438e31176d92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324478} {:answer/id #uuid "abecbcfc-c2c9-55b7-a6bd-b251b89a9b98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325432}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f1e464f-a9e3-5cb7-9c86-438e31176d92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324478} :answer-field/id #uuid "521acea1-0e27-577c-b181-7478a17ba862" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326360}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "063b2ff3-4387-5a0a-88b2-8a1c68641ed4" :question/math-academy-id "q-40516" :question/problem "Let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Find $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)$, where $x=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $y=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nThe linear transformation $Q$ being orthogonal is equivalent to the following:\n\n- The matrix $Q$ of transformation $Q$ relative to any orthonormal basis (including the standard basis) of ${R}^{n}$ is orthogonal.\n\n- $||Q(x)||=||x||.$\n\n- If $x⊥y$ then $Q(x)⊥Q(y)$ for any $x,y\\in {R}^{n}.$ In general, $Q$ preserves the angle between any two vectors.\n\n- $Q$ maps any orthogonal (orthonormal) set into an orthogonal (orthonormal) set.\n\nIn this case, since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x)\\cdot Q(y) & =x\\cdot y \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-2)+(-1)\\cdot 1+1\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
